A real defect found via new test coverage while doing PRODUCT_RELEASE_PLAN_AR.md
Phase 1 (moving netlink.link's decision logic behind internal/ports +
internal/analyze, per §4.1).

THE DEFECT (in the shipped internal/collect/netlink/link_linux.go, before this
change): the down-transition required BOTH admin_up and oper_up to already be
false:

    case (prev.adminUp || prevOperUp) && !cur.adminUp && !curOperUp:

A real NIC (or, as it turns out, a veth whose peer goes down) losing carrier
while remaining administratively enabled - admin_up stays true, only oper_up
drops - could never satisfy this condition, so no link.down event, no flap
detection, and no severity distinction ever fired for it. Every existing lab
scenario and every prior test toggles interfaces with "ip link set ... down",
which changes admin state too, so this was never exercised - the project's own
comment describing this feature ("Administrative state is distinguished from
operational state... reporting both as 'interface down' is what makes
existing tools useless") was not actually true for the most realistic real-
world case it names.

THE FIX: internal/analyze/interface.go now compares "effectively up"
(admin_up AND oper_up) symmetrically in both directions, so either signal
alone going down is sufficient to report link.down (with the correct cause),
and either alone recovering is sufficient to report link.up. Full unit
coverage in internal/analyze/interface_test.go, including
TestCarrierLossWithAdminStillUpIsCauseCarrier, which fails against the old
logic and passes against the fix.

LIVE PROOF, not just a unit test: re-running the existing, unmodified
lab/inject.sh all gate (the same 14-scenario run used throughout
docs/evidence/) with the fixed binaries produced a SECOND link.down event that
never appeared in any prior run of this exact gate:

$ netrewind events --db /tmp/netrewind-lab/events.db --last 20m --kind link.down
TIME          KIND       SUBJECT  SEV   DETAIL
15:52:46.402  link.down  nrlab1   warn  admin_up=false cause=administrative oper_state=down
15:53:52.653  link.down  nrlab0   warn  admin_up=true  cause=carrier       oper_state=down

The second row is real and organic, not constructed for this test: it comes
from the existing measured_loss scenario, which brings down H1's nrp0 - the
veth peer of nrlab0. That leaves nrlab0's own admin_up untouched (true) while
its oper state follows its peer down, which is exactly the carrier-loss shape
the old code could not see. Every previous run of this same gate
(docs/evidence/04-full-lab-gate.log) recorded only ONE link.down for this
scenario set; this run recorded two, and the second one is real information
that was previously and silently lost, from a scenario this project already
runs on every commit.

Full re-run of the gate: PASS, all 14 event kinds recorded, all 8 correlation
rules concluded - no regression, only the additional, previously-missing
event.

Full test suites, both platforms, after this change:
- Windows: gofmt clean, go vet clean, go test ./... all pass.
- Linux (WSL2 netrewind-lab): gofmt clean, go vet clean,
  CGO_ENABLED=1 go test -race ./... all pass, including a coverage test
  (internal/event/coverage_test.go: TestEveryKindIsProducedOrReserved) that
  had to be told about the new internal/analyze package - its whole purpose
  is catching an event kind quietly becoming unproduced, and moving the
  producing code without updating its scan list would have been exactly
  that failure mode.
